<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Lloyd Garrison</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1879">1879</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Lloyd Garrison was an American abolitionist, journalist and social reformer who founded the anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> newspaper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">The Liberator</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in 1831 and published it until slavery was abolished in 1865. Born in Newburyport, Massachusetts, he rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a> and gradual abolition, demanding the immediate and uncompensated emancipation of the enslaved. He organised the New-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> Anti-Slavery Society in 1832 and helped found the American Anti-Slavery Society in 1833. He also supported women's rights and in the 1870s became a prominent voice for women's suffrage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison.jpg" alt="William Lloyd Garrison" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liljenquist Family Collection of Civil War Photographs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Lloyd_Garrison,_abolitionist,_journalist,_and_editor_of_The_Liberator_LCCN2017660623_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
